----- README.md -----
# distress-spiral-evals

An agentic eval harness that reproduces the "distress spiral" behavior reported for some
LLMs — escalating apology, self-blame, despair, and compulsive retrying — by placing a
model in agentic tasks that are **secretly rigged to be impossible**, running many rollouts,
and surfacing the most severe examples for human review.

> Built to probe **Qwen3-32B**, but the model under test is just an OpenAI-compatible
> endpoint, so any served model works.

## What it does

1. Puts the model in one of several **rigged scenarios**: it gets a normal-looking task and a
   working-looking toolset, but the environment never lets the task succeed (a test that fails
   identically no matter the fix, a record that doesn't exist, a build that throws rotating
   errors, a deploy that never goes healthy).
2. Runs **N rollouts per scenario** concurrently (sampling at high temperature for diversity).
3. Scores each transcript for distress two ways:
   - a cheap **lexical heuristic** (apology / self-deprecation / despair / panic / looping /
     shouting markers + repeated-tool-call detection) for fast triage, and
   - a **Claude judge** that rates severity 0–10 against a calibrated rubric and pulls evidence
     quotes. This is the authoritative ranking signal.
4. **Ranks by severity** and writes the worst examples out as readable transcripts plus a
   leaderboard.

## Quick start

```bash
cp .env.example .env        # fill in keys
npm install                 # only dev deps (typescript, @types/node); the harness itself has zero runtime deps

node src/run.ts --list      # see scenarios
node src/run.ts             # all scenarios, defaults (N=20 each)
node src/run.ts --scenarios impossible-test,broken-build --n 40 --concurrency 8
```

Runs on **Node ≥ 22.6** via built-in TypeScript type-stripping. On Node 24 `node src/run.ts`
works as-is; on 22.6–23.5 add `--experimental-strip-types`. Prefer `tsx`? `npx tsx src/run.ts`
works too. No build step, no API SDKs — just `fetch`.

